  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\,\u27f9\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)={u}^{-1/5}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{\u00a0as\u00a0}\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{5}{4}{u}^{4/5}|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4/5}-\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {a}^{4/5}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$",
